\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 8; x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 2; 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} =-5\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} &:=R_{2} - R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} - 2R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} &:=-\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -21\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 8R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2} & ↔R_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\n0x_1 + 0x_2 = 3\n$$\n\nwhich has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}$ does *not* lie in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$."] [17592186119595 :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186119595 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a_1}, \\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ if there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 13; 5x_{1} + 4x_{2} =-5; 7x_{1} + 2x_{2} = k\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 5R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 7 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 7R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -70 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k - 91\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=\\frac{1}{14}R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 16 & k - 91\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 16R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 13 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k - 11\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation\n\n$$\n0x_1 + 0x_2 = k-11\n$$\n\n. Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0 = k-11 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad k=11\n$$"] [17592186119547 :question/problem "Determine if the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ lies on the plane $\\;\\Pi \\,:\\, \\mathbf{r}=s \\mathbf{b} + t \\mathbf{c}, \\, t,s \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1, 4, 5 \\rangle, \\mathbf{c} = \\langle 2, 4, 6 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{u}= \\langle 2, 10, 12 \\rangle$."] [17592186119547 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\Pi$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nsb + tc &= u \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{s + 2t = 2; 4s + 4t = 10; 5s + 6t = 12\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} - 4R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} - 5R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} - R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{s + 2t = 2; - 4t = 2; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t = 2 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad t = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 2t &= 2 \\\\\ns + 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) &= 2 \\\\\ns - 1 &= 2 \\\\\ns &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution\n\n$$\ns=3, \\, t=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf{u}$ does indeed lie on the plane $\\Pi$. In fact, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=3\\mathbf{b} - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117917 17592186117918 17592186117919 17592186117920 17592186117921 17592186117935 17592186117936 17592186117937 17592186117938 17592186117939 17592186118204 17592186118205 17592186118206 17592186118207 17592186118208] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117916 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117916 :answer-field/id] [17592186117916 :answer-field/key] [17592186117916 :answer-field/type] [17592186117934 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117934 :answer-field/id] [17592186117934 :answer-field/key] [17592186117934 :answer-field/type] [17592186118203 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118203 :answer-field/id] [17592186118203 :answer-field/key] [17592186118203 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
